From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:48:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Connectivity: do not throw on ENOSYS from a kernel with no
 eBPF

WiFi on ether 20.0 completes the WPA handshake and gets a full DHCP lease,
then fails to provision:

  E Netd    : no such netId 100
  W ConnectivityService: Exception adding interface: ServiceSpecificException:
              Machine is not on the network (code 64)
  D ConnectivityService: Setting DNS servers for network 100 to []

netd never created the network, so there is nothing for the routes or DNS to
attach to. netd is not at fault -- ConnectivityService never called
networkCreate(), because the setup path threw first:

  E NetworkStats: IllegalStateException: IOException: ServiceSpecificException:
                  Unable to swap active stats map: Function not implemented

maybeThrow() converts any non-zero errno into a ServiceSpecificException, and
all ten of its call sites are BPF operations. On a 3.10 kernel there is no
bpf() syscall, so they return ENOSYS every time, forever -- 113 attempts in a
single boot by WifiHandlerThread, NetworkPolicy, jobscheduler and others.

ENOSYS here is not an error condition to propagate, it is a permanent property
of the kernel. So log it and continue, the same reasoning applied to BpfMap's
constructor: the BPF-backed features are unavailable either way, and taking the
caller down with them is what actually breaks the device.

Complements the existing BpfHandler patch (socket tagging) and the BpfMap
constructor fix (map open). Same class, third location.
---
 service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java b/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
index c006bc605..be83c328e 100644
--- a/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
+++ b/service/src/com/android/server/BpfNetMaps.java
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 
 package com.android.server;
 
+import static android.system.OsConstants.ENOSYS;
 import static android.system.OsConstants.EOPNOTSUPP;
 
 import android.net.INetd;
@@ -67,6 +68,17 @@ public class BpfNetMaps {
     }
 
     private void maybeThrow(final int err, final String msg) {
+        if (err == ENOSYS) {
+            // No bpf() syscall at all: pre-4.x kernels predate it entirely, so every
+            // one of these calls will fail this way for the life of the device. Throwing
+            // aborts whatever operation the caller was part of -- on msm8992 that meant
+            // ConnectivityService never reached networkCreate(), so netd had no network
+            // to attach routes to and WiFi could not provision despite a valid DHCP
+            // lease. Degrade instead: the BPF-backed features (per-UID accounting, the
+            // firewall chains) are unavailable regardless, but the caller can continue.
+            Log.w(TAG, msg + ": " + Os.strerror(err) + " -- kernel has no eBPF, continuing");
+            return;
+        }
         if (err != 0) {
             throw new ServiceSpecificException(err, msg + ": " + Os.strerror(err));
         }
